invariantRing -- the ring of invariants of a group action
*********************************************************

  * Usage: 
        invariantRing G, R^G
  * Inputs:
      * G, an instance of the type *note GroupAction: GroupAction,
      * R, a *note polynomial ring: (Macaulay2Doc)PolynomialRing,, on which the
        group acts
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note DegreeBound: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeBound=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value infinity, degree bound for invariants of finite
        groups
      * *note Strategy: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value "Default", choose the strategy for computing
        invariants
      * *note UseCoefficientRing:
        invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmUseCoefficientRing=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value false, option to compute invariants over the given
        coefficient ring
      * *note UsePolyhedra: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmUsePolyhedra=>_pd_pd_pd_rp
        , => ..., default value false, use Polyhedra package for invariants of
        tori
  * Outputs:
      * an instance of the type *note RingOfInvariants: RingOfInvariants,, the
        ring of invariants of the given group action

Description
===========

This function is provided by the package *note InvariantRing: Top,. 

The following example defines an action of a two-dimensional torus on a
four-dimensional vector space with a basis of weight vectors whose weights are
the columns of the input matrix.

+---------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x_1..x_4]                  |
|                                       |
|o1 = R                                 |
|                                       |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                    |
+---------------------------------------+
|i2 : W = matrix{{0,1,-1,1},{1,0,-1,-1}}|
|                                       |
|o2 = | 0 1 -1 1  |                     |
|     | 1 0 -1 -1 |                     |
|                                       |
|              2       4                |
|o2 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                 |
+---------------------------------------+
|i3 : T = diagonalAction(W, R)          |
|                                       |
|             * 2                       |
|o3 = R <- (QQ )  via                   |
|                                       |
|     | 0 1 -1 1  |                     |
|     | 1 0 -1 -1 |                     |
|                                       |
|o3 : DiagonalAction                    |
+---------------------------------------+
|i4 : S = invariantRing T               |
|                                       |
|o4 =             2                     |
|     QQ[x x x , x x x ]                |
|         1 2 3   1 3 4                 |
|                                       |
|o4 : RingOfInvariants                  |
+---------------------------------------+

The algebra generators for the ring of invariants are computed upon
initialization by the method *note invariants: invariants,.

Alternatively, we can use the following shortcut to construct a ring of
invariants.

+-----------------------+
|i5 : S = R^T           |
|                       |
|o5 =             2     |
|     QQ[x x x , x x x ]|
|         1 2 3   1 3 4 |
|                       |
|o5 : RingOfInvariants  |
+-----------------------+

Caveat
======

By default, the invariants of a diagonal group action are computed over an
infinite extension of the ground field specified by the user over which the
action is defined. Provided the action is defined, it is possible to compute
invariants literally over the specified ground field in prime characteristic
using the option *note UseCoefficientRing:
invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmUseCoefficientRing=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,.

Ways to use invariantRing:
==========================

  * "invariantRing(GroupAction)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note invariantRing: invariantRing, is a *note method function with
options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

