invariants(DiagonalAction) -- computes the generating invariants of a group action
**********************************************************************************

  * Function: *note invariants: invariants,
  * Usage: 
        invariants D
  * Inputs:
      * D, an instance of the type *note DiagonalAction: DiagonalAction,, a
        diagonal action on a polynomial ring
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note DegreeBound: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeBound=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value infinity, degree bound for invariants of finite
        groups
      * *note DegreeLimit: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value {}, GB option for invariants
      * *note Strategy: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value "Default", choose the strategy for computing
        invariants
      * *note SubringLimit: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmSubringLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp
        , => ..., default value infinity, GB option for invariants
      * *note UseCoefficientRing:
        invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmUseCoefficientRing=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value false, option to compute invariants over the given
        coefficient ring
      * *note UsePolyhedra: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmUsePolyhedra=>_pd_pd_pd_rp
        , => ..., default value false, use Polyhedra package for invariants of
        tori
  * Outputs:
      * L, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a minimal set of generating
        invariants for the group action

Description
===========

This function is provided by the package *note InvariantRing: Top,. It
implements an algorithm to compute a minimal set of generating monomial
invariants for a diagonal action of an abelian group $(k^*)^r \times
\mathbb{Z}/d_1 \times \cdots \times \mathbb{Z}/d_g$  on a polynomial ring  $R =
k[x_1, \dots, x_n]$. Saying the action is diagonal means that
$(t_1,\ldots,t_r) \in (k^*)^r$  acts by $$(t_1,\ldots,t_r) \cdot x_j =
t_1^{w_{1,j}}\cdots t_r^{w_{r,j}} x_j$$ for some integers  $w_{i,j}$  and the
generators  $u_1, \dots, u_g$  of the cyclic abelian factors act by $$u_i \cdot
x_j = \zeta_i^{w_{r+i,j}} x_j$$ for  $\zeta_i$  a primitive  $d_i$-th root of
unity. The integers $w_{i,j}$ comprise the weight matrix W. In other words, the
$j$ -th column of W is the weight vector of $x_j$.

The algorithm combines a modified version of an algorithm for tori due to
Derksen and Kemper which can be found in:

  * Derksen, H. & Kemper, G. (2015).Computational Invariant Theory. Heidelberg:
    Springer. pp 159-164

together with an algorithm for finite abelian groups due to Gandini which can
be found in:

  * Gandini, F. Ideals of Subspace Arrangements. Thesis (Ph.D.)-University of
    Michigan. 2019. ISBN: 978-1392-76291-2. pp 29-34.

Version 2.5 includes a faster algorithm to compute invariants of elementary
abelian $p$-groups which is used by default when possible, i.e., when there is
no torus action, all cyclic factors have the same prime order, and the weight
matrix has maximal rank. For more information, see:

  * Arasha S., Cassell M., Dolorfino M., Gandini F., Novak G., Qin D., and
    Strom S. (2026). An algorithm for invariants of elementary abelian groups.
    arXiv:2605.19158 (see https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19158 )

Here is an example of a one-dimensional torus acting on a two-dimensional
vector space:

+-----------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x_1,x_2]         |
|                             |
|o1 = R                       |
|                             |
|o1 : PolynomialRing          |
+-----------------------------+
|i2 : W = matrix{{1,-1}}      |
|                             |
|o2 = | 1 -1 |                |
|                             |
|              1       2      |
|o2 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ       |
+-----------------------------+
|i3 : T = diagonalAction(W, R)|
|                             |
|            *                |
|o3 = R <- QQ  via            |
|                             |
|     | 1 -1 |                |
|                             |
|o3 : DiagonalAction          |
+-----------------------------+
|i4 : invariants T            |
|                             |
|o4 = {x x }                  |
|       1 2                   |
|                             |
|o4 : List                    |
+-----------------------------+

Here is an example of a product of two cyclic groups of order 3 acting on a
three-dimensional vector space:

+---------------------------------+
|i5 : R = QQ[x_1..x_3]            |
|                                 |
|o5 = R                           |
|                                 |
|o5 : PolynomialRing              |
+---------------------------------+
|i6 : d = {3,3}                   |
|                                 |
|o6 = {3, 3}                      |
|                                 |
|o6 : List                        |
+---------------------------------+
|i7 : W = matrix{{1,0,1},{0,1,1}} |
|                                 |
|o7 = | 1 0 1 |                   |
|     | 0 1 1 |                   |
|                                 |
|              2       3          |
|o7 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ           |
+---------------------------------+
|i8 : A = diagonalAction(W, d, R) |
|                                 |
|o8 = R <- ZZ/3 x ZZ/3 via        |
|                                 |
|     | 1 0 1 |                   |
|     | 0 1 1 |                   |
|                                 |
|o8 : DiagonalAction              |
+---------------------------------+
|i9 : invariants A                |
|                                 |
|       3   3   3       2   2 2   |
|o9 = {x , x , x , x x x , x x x }|
|       3   2   1   1 2 3   1 2 3 |
|                                 |
|o9 : List                        |
+---------------------------------+

The example above uses the new algorithm for elementary abelian $p$-groups
introduced in version 2.4. To call the older general-purpose algorithm, use the
option Strategy=>"DerksenGandini".

+----------------------------------------------+
|i10 : invariants(A,Strategy=>"DerksenGandini")|
|                                              |
|        3   3   3       2   2 2               |
|o10 = {x , x , x , x x x , x x x }            |
|        3   2   1   1 2 3   1 2 3             |
|                                              |
|o10 : List                                    |
+----------------------------------------------+

Here is an example of a diagonal action by the product of a two-dimensional
torus with a cyclic group of order 3 acting on a two-dimensional vector space:

+--------------------------------------+
|i11 : R = QQ[x_1, x_2]                |
|                                      |
|o11 = R                               |
|                                      |
|o11 : PolynomialRing                  |
+--------------------------------------+
|i12 : d = {3}                         |
|                                      |
|o12 = {3}                             |
|                                      |
|o12 : List                            |
+--------------------------------------+
|i13 : W1 = matrix{{1,-1}, {-1,1}}     |
|                                      |
|o13 = | 1  -1 |                       |
|      | -1 1  |                       |
|                                      |
|               2       2              |
|o13 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ               |
+--------------------------------------+
|i14 : W2 = matrix {{1,0}}             |
|                                      |
|o14 = | 1 0 |                         |
|                                      |
|               1       2              |
|o14 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ               |
+--------------------------------------+
|i15 : D = diagonalAction(W1, W2, d, R)|
|                                      |
|              * 2                     |
|o15 = R <- (QQ )  x ZZ/3 via          |
|                                      |
|      (| 1  -1 |, | 1 0 |)            |
|       | -1 1  |                      |
|                                      |
|o15 : DiagonalAction                  |
+--------------------------------------+
|i16 : invariants D                    |
|                                      |
|        3 3                           |
|o16 = {x x }                          |
|        1 2                           |
|                                      |
|o16 : List                            |
+--------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

By default, the invariants are computed over an infinite extension of the
ground field specified by the user over which the action is defined.  Provided
the action is defined, it is possible to compute invariants literally over the
specified ground field in prime characteristic using the option *note
UseCoefficientRing: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmUseCoefficientRing=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,.

See also
========

  * *note diagonalAction: diagonalAction, -- diagonal group action via weights
  * *note invariantRing: invariantRing, -- the ring of invariants of a group
    action
  * *note isInvariant: isInvariant, -- check whether a polynomial is invariant
    under a group action

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note invariants(DiagonalAction): invariants_lpDiagonalAction_rp, --
    computes the generating invariants of a group action
