invariants(LinearlyReductiveAction) -- invariant generators of Hilbert ideal
****************************************************************************

  * Function: *note invariants: invariants,
  * Usage: 
        invariants V
  * Inputs:
      * V, an instance of the type *note LinearlyReductiveAction:
        LinearlyReductiveAction,
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note DegreeBound: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeBound=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value infinity, degree bound for invariants of finite
        groups
      * *note DegreeLimit: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,
        => ..., default value {}, GB option for invariants
      * *note Strategy: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmStrategy=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, =>
        ..., default value "Default", choose the strategy for computing
        invariants
      * *note SubringLimit: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmSubringLimit=>_pd_pd_pd_rp
        , => ..., default value infinity, GB option for invariants
      * *note UseCoefficientRing:
        invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmUseCoefficientRing=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value false, option to compute invariants over the given
        coefficient ring
      * *note UsePolyhedra: invariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmUsePolyhedra=>_pd_pd_pd_rp
        , => ..., default value false, use Polyhedra package for invariants of
        tori
  * Outputs:
      * L, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of invariants generating the
        Hilbert ideal

Description
===========

This function is provided by the package *note InvariantRing: Top,

When called on a linearly reductive group action and a degree, this function
returns a list of generators for the Hilbert ideal that are also invariant
under the action. This function computes the Hilbert ideal first using *note
hilbertIdeal: hilbertIdeal, then finds invariant generators degree by degree
using *note invariants(LinearlyReductiveAction,ZZ):
invariants_lpLinearlyReductiveAction_cmZZ_rp,.

The next example constructs a cyclic group of order 2 as a set of two affine
points. Then it introduces an action of this group on a polynomial ring in two
variables and computes the Hilbert ideal. The action permutes the variables of
the polynomial ring.

+------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : S = QQ[z]                                        |
|                                                      |
|o1 = S                                                |
|                                                      |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                                   |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal(z^2 - 1)                               |
|                                                      |
|            2                                         |
|o2 = ideal(z  - 1)                                    |
|                                                      |
|o2 : Ideal of S                                       |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : M = matrix{{(z+1)/2, (1-z)/2},{(1-z)/2, (z+1)/2}}|
|                                                      |
|o3 = | 1/2z+1/2  -1/2z+1/2 |                          |
|     | -1/2z+1/2 1/2z+1/2  |                          |
|                                                      |
|             2      2                                 |
|o3 : Matrix S  <-- S                                  |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : sub(M,z=>1),sub(M,z=>-1)                         |
|                                                      |
|o4 = (| 1 0 |, | 0 1 |)                               |
|      | 0 1 |  | 1 0 |                                |
|                                                      |
|o4 : Sequence                                         |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : R = QQ[x,y]                                      |
|                                                      |
|o5 = R                                                |
|                                                      |
|o5 : PolynomialRing                                   |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : V = linearlyReductiveAction(I, M, R)             |
|                                                      |
|                   2                                  |
|o6 = R <- S/ideal(z  - 1) via                         |
|                                                      |
|     | 1/2z+1/2  -1/2z+1/2 |                          |
|     | -1/2z+1/2 1/2z+1/2  |                          |
|                                                      |
|o6 : LinearlyReductiveAction                          |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : H = hilbertIdeal V                               |
|                                                      |
|                    2                                 |
|o7 = ideal (x + y, y )                                |
|                                                      |
|o7 : Ideal of R                                       |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : invariants V                                     |
|                                                      |
|o8 = {x + y, x*y}                                     |
|                                                      |
|o8 : List                                             |
+------------------------------------------------------+

The algorithm for the Hilbert ideal performs an elimination using Groebner
bases. The options *note DegreeLimit: (Macaulay2Doc)DegreeLimit, and *note
SubringLimit: (Macaulay2Doc)SubringLimit, are standard *note gb:
(Macaulay2Doc)gb, options that can be used to interrupt the computation before
it is complete, yielding a partial list of invariant generators for the Hilbert
ideal.

Caveat
======

Both *note hilbertIdeal: hilbertIdeal, and *note
invariants(LinearlyReductiveAction,ZZ):
invariants_lpLinearlyReductiveAction_cmZZ_rp, require Groebner bases
computations, which could lead to long running times. It might be helpful to
run these functions separately.

See also
========

  * *note hilbertIdeal: hilbertIdeal, -- compute generators for the Hilbert
    ideal
  * *note invariants: invariants, -- computes the generating invariants of a
    group action

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note invariants(LinearlyReductiveAction):
    invariants_lpLinearlyReductiveAction_rp, -- invariant generators of Hilbert
    ideal
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

