primaryInvariants(...,DegreeVector=>...) -- an optional argument for primaryInvariants that finds invariants of certain degrees
*******************************************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        primaryInvariants G
  * Inputs:
      * G, an instance of the type *note FiniteGroupAction: FiniteGroupAction,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,,  consisting of a homogeneous system
        of parameters (hsop) for the invariant ring of the group action

Description
===========

                                                                          
By default, *note primaryInvariants: primaryInvariants, uses an optimising
                                                                          
                                                                             
algorithm which tests for the existence of a homogeneous system of parameters
                                                                             
                                                                              
(hsop) (f ,...,f ) with positive degrees corresponding to (d ,...,d ) in *note
         1      n                                           1      n
                     n
ZZ: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ, . If it is known that a hsop exists for a certain
collection of degrees, this can be assigned, as a *note List:
(Macaulay2Doc)List,, to the optional argument *note DegreeVector:
primaryInvariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeVector=>_pd_pd_pd_rp,. *note
primaryInvariants: primaryInvariants, will then output a hsop corresponding to
this list of degrees. If however no such hsop exists, *note primaryInvariants:
primaryInvariants, outputs an error message.

Note that the *note List: (Macaulay2Doc)List, assigned to *note DegreeVector:
primaryInvariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegreeVector=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, is ignored if *note
Dade: primaryInvariants_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDade=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, is set to *note true:
(Macaulay2Doc)true,.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : A=matrix{{0,1,0},{0,0,1},{1,0,0}};                                |
|                                                                       |
|              3       3                                                |
|o1 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : B=matrix{{0,1,0},{1,0,0},{0,0,1}};                                |
|                                                                       |
|              3       3                                                |
|o2 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : S3=finiteAction({A,B},QQ[x,y,z])                                  |
|                                                                       |
|o3 = QQ[x..z] <- <| 0 1 0 |, | 0 1 0 |>                                |
|                  | 0 0 1 |  | 1 0 0 |                                 |
|                  | 1 0 0 |  | 0 0 1 |                                 |
|                                                                       |
|o3 : FiniteGroupAction                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : primaryInvariants(S3,DegreeVector=>{3,3,4})                       |
|                                                                       |
|       3    3    3   2       2    2     2       2      2   4    4    4 |
|o4 = {x  + y  + z , x y + x*y  + x z + y z + x*z  + y*z , x  + y  + z }|
|                                                                       |
|o4 : List                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

                                                                           
Currently users can only use *note primaryInvariants: primaryInvariants, to
                                                                             
calculate a hsop for the invariant ring over a finite field by using the Dade
                                                                      
algorithm. Users should enter the finite field as a *note GaloisField:
                                                                    
(Macaulay2Doc)GaloisField, or a quotient field of the form *note ZZ:
                                                                       
(Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,/p and are advised to ensure that the ground field has
                            n-1
cardinality greater than |G|   , where n is the number of variables in the
polynomial ring R. Using a ground field smaller than this runs the risk of the
algorithm getting stuck in an infinite loop; *note primaryInvariants:
primaryInvariants, displays a warning message asking the user whether they wish
to continue with the computation in this case. See *note hsop algorithms: hsop
algorithms, for a discussion on the Dade algorithm.

